# ChatBots — Caddy front end
#
# Caddy is the public face: it listens on 7788, serves the web interface, and forwards
# /api/* to the Swift engine behind it. The engine itself never faces outwards.
#
#   browser ──▶ :7788 Caddy ──┬──▶ /            static files from ./web
#                              └──▶ /api/*      127.0.0.1:7789  (the Swift engine)
#
# HTTP only, deliberately. This is a local chat app: it is not exposed beyond the local
# network unless something else is put in front of it, and the engine behind Caddy already
# binds to loopback. Automatic HTTPS would try to obtain a certificate for a name that is not
# public and fail noisily for no benefit.
#
# Run it with:  caddy run --config Caddyfile
# Or let tools/start.sh manage it.

{
	# No admin API: nothing needs to reconfigure a running instance, and leaving it on
	# would open another port.
	admin off
	# Keep the log on stderr so the start script shows one stream.
	log {
		output stderr
		level WARN
	}
}

# The site address below carries no host, and that is what decides the listener: Caddy listens
# on ":7788" — **every** interface (0.0.0.0 and ::). The host part of a site address only
# chooses which Host header a request must carry; it does not narrow the listener. So the
# website, and with it the whole unauthenticated /api/* surface, is reachable by anyone who can
# reach this port on the network: every kept conversation, the run controls, the roster and the
# share pages. That is deliberate — it is what lets a phone on the same Wi-Fi use the interface
# — and the engine behind Caddy stays on loopback regardless.
#
# To bind this Mac only, run `tools/start.sh --local-only`. It generates a copy of this file
# with `bind 127.0.0.1` inserted inside the site block below, which is the directive that
# actually picks the listening interface — a site address of `127.0.0.1:7788` would not, it
# would still listen on ":7788". See "Using the website" in the wiki.
http://:7788 {
	# tools/start.sh --local-only inserts `bind 127.0.0.1` here. Absent, this site listens
	# on every interface, by design.
	encode zstd gzip

	# The engine. `flush_interval -1` disables response buffering, which server-sent events
	# need: without it Caddy would hold the stream and the live transcript would never
	# appear until the turn finished — the exact thing the feed exists to avoid.
	handle /api/* {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:7789 {
			flush_interval -1
			# Loopback only, so no need to pass a host header through.
			header_up Host {upstream_hostport}
		}
	}

	# The interface itself.
	handle {
		root * ./web
		file_server
		# A reload during development must not serve a stale page.
		header Cache-Control "no-store"
	}

	# Anything not found is a mistake rather than a client route, so Caddy's default 404 is
	# the honest answer.
}
